// Authored content for 3.1.5.3 Haemoglobin and the transport of oxygen. Spec statements s1–s6 (OxfordAQA 9610 v5.1).
window.BIO = window.BIO || {}; window.BIO.content = window.BIO.content || {};
(function () {
  const A = window.BIO.gasxArt, M = window.BIO.gasxModel;
  const R = "3.1.5.3";
  const P = (slot) => window.BIO.views.photo(slot);
  const pct = (p, v, c) => Math.round(100 * M.sat(p, v, c));
  window.BIO.content[R] = {
    status: "reviewed",
    banner: "rbc-sem", bannerText: "Haemoglobin: a protein tuned to load oxygen where there is plenty and let go where it is needed.",
    keywords: ["haemoglobin", "red blood cell", "erythrocyte", "oxygen", "dissociation curve", "partial pressure", "saturation", "loading", "unloading", "affinity", "cooperative", "Bohr effect", "carbon dioxide", "haem", "iron", "globin", "quaternary", "circulation", "artery", "vein", "capillary", "aorta", "vena cava", "pulmonary", "hepatic", "coronary", "double circulation", "fetal haemoglobin", "llama", "lugworm", "adaptation"],
    notes: () => `
<div class="notice"><strong>What the specification requires (3.1.5.3).</strong> (1) The general pattern of blood circulation in a mammal through arteries, capillaries and veins; names required <em>only</em> for the coronary arteries and the vessels entering and leaving the heart and liver. (2) The roles of haemoglobin and red blood cells in transporting oxygen, including the oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve. (3) The effect of carbon dioxide concentration on the transport of oxygen by haemoglobin. (4) Haemoglobins are a group of chemically similar molecules found in many organisms; each has polypeptide globin chains, each associated with an iron-containing haem group. (5) "Many animals are adapted to their environments by possessing different types of haemoglobin with different oxygen-transporting properties." (6) Students should be able to "relate the oxygen transporting properties of different types of haemoglobin to the environment and way of life of the organism concerned." <em>Not required by OxfordAQA here:</em> the structure of the heart and the cardiac cycle (3.2.6), the detailed structure of vessel walls (3.2.6), the renal vessels, carbon dioxide transport chemistry, the names of fetal globin chains. <span class="badge teal">required</span> <span class="badge">supporting</span> <span class="badge amber">extension</span></div>
<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s1">1. The general pattern of circulation <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Mammals have a <strong>closed, double circulation</strong>: blood stays inside vessels, and passes through the heart twice in each complete circuit. The <strong>pulmonary circuit</strong> carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs and back to the left side; the <strong>systemic circuit</strong> carries it from the left side to the rest of the body and back to the right side. Keeping the circuits separate lets the systemic circuit run at a higher pressure than the delicate lung capillaries could withstand, and prevents oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mixing.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Arteries</strong> carry blood <em>away from</em> the heart (under high pressure); they branch into arterioles and then <strong>capillaries</strong>, where exchange with the tissues takes place through walls one cell thick; capillaries join into venules and <strong>veins</strong>, which return blood <em>to</em> the heart at low pressure.</li>
<li>Arteries usually carry oxygenated blood and veins deoxygenated, with the pulmonary vessels as the exceptions: the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs and the pulmonary vein returns oxygenated blood.</li></ul>
<table><tr><th>Vessel (names required)</th><th>From → to</th><th>Blood</th></tr>
<tr><th>Vena cava</th><td>Body → right atrium</td><td>Deoxygenated</td></tr>
<tr><th>Pulmonary artery</th><td>Right ventricle → lungs</td><td>Deoxygenated</td></tr>
<tr><th>Pulmonary vein</th><td>Lungs → left atrium</td><td>Oxygenated</td></tr>
<tr><th>Aorta</th><td>Left ventricle → body</td><td>Oxygenated</td></tr>
<tr><th>Coronary arteries</th><td>Aorta (just above the heart) → heart muscle</td><td>Oxygenated</td></tr>
<tr><th>Hepatic artery</th><td>Aorta → liver</td><td>Oxygenated</td></tr>
<tr><th>Hepatic portal vein</th><td>Gut (intestines) → liver</td><td>Deoxygenated, rich in absorbed nutrients</td></tr>
<tr><th>Hepatic vein</th><td>Liver → vena cava</td><td>Deoxygenated</td></tr></table>
<p><span class="badge">supporting</span> The hepatic portal vein is unusual: it connects two capillary beds (gut and liver) so that everything absorbed from food passes through the liver first. The coronary arteries matter because the heart muscle cannot take oxygen from the blood in its chambers; blockage of a coronary artery starves part of the wall of oxygen (3.2.6).</p>
${A.circulation()}
<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s2 ${R}.s4">2. Red blood cells and haemoglobin <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<div class="grid grid-2">${P("rbc-sem")}<ul><li>Oxygen dissolves only slightly in plasma; nearly all of it is carried bound to <strong>haemoglobin</strong> inside <strong>red blood cells</strong> (erythrocytes).</li><li>Red cells are <strong>biconcave discs</strong> about 7–8 µm across with <strong>no nucleus</strong> (and no mitochondria) in mammals: more room for haemoglobin, a large surface area to volume ratio and a short diffusion distance to every molecule. They are small and flexible enough to pass through capillaries in single file, close to the wall.</li><li>Keeping haemoglobin inside cells keeps the plasma's water potential and viscosity normal.</li></ul></div>
<h3>Haemoglobin structure <span class="badge teal">required</span></h3>
<p>Haemoglobins are a group of <strong>chemically similar</strong> proteins found in many organisms (mammals, birds, fish, many invertebrates, even some plants and bacteria). Each molecule consists of <strong>polypeptide globin chains</strong>, and each chain is associated with an <strong>iron-containing haem group</strong>. The iron ion (Fe²⁺) in each haem can bind one molecule of oxygen, reversibly: haemoglobin + O₂ ⇌ oxyhaemoglobin. Adult human haemoglobin has four chains (two α and two β; quaternary structure, 3.1.1.4) and four haems, so one molecule carries up to four O₂ molecules. <span class="badge">supporting</span> Haem is a prosthetic group (a non-protein part), not an amino acid; the chain surrounding it decides how readily the iron binds oxygen, which is why different haemoglobins behave differently.</p>
${A.hbMolecule()}
${P("haemoglobin-structure")}
<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s2">3. Loading, unloading and the dissociation curve <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>How much oxygen haemoglobin carries depends on the <strong>partial pressure of oxygen (pO₂)</strong> around it: the share of the total gas pressure due to oxygen, measured in kPa, which is in effect the concentration of oxygen. Where pO₂ is high (lungs, ≈ 13 kPa) haemoglobin <strong>loads</strong> (associates with) oxygen; where pO₂ is low (respiring tissues, ≈ 5 kPa or less) it <strong>unloads</strong> (dissociates). The <strong>oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve</strong> plots the percentage <strong>saturation</strong> of haemoglobin with oxygen (y) against pO₂ (x).</p>
${A.odc({ variants: ["adult"], width: 620 })}
<p>The curve is <strong>sigmoidal (S-shaped)</strong> because of <strong>cooperative binding</strong>: the first oxygen molecule binds with difficulty, but binding changes the shape (tertiary and quaternary structure) of the protein, making the remaining haem groups more accessible, so the second and third bind more easily (the steep part); the fourth is harder again because there is only one site left (the curve levels off).</p>
<ul><li><strong>Flat top (high pO₂):</strong> in the lungs haemoglobin is almost fully saturated (about 97 % at 13 kPa in the model), and saturation changes little even if alveolar pO₂ falls somewhat: a safety margin.</li><li><strong>Steep middle (3–8 kPa):</strong> the range found in respiring tissues. A small fall in pO₂ causes a large fall in saturation, so haemoglobin releases a lot of oxygen exactly where it is needed: at 5 kPa the model gives about ${pct(5, "adult")} % saturation, a fall of ${pct(13, "adult") - pct(5, "adult")} percentage points from the lungs, so about ${Math.round(100 * (pct(13, "adult") - pct(5, "adult")) / pct(13, "adult"))} % of the oxygen loaded in the lungs has been unloaded; very active tissue at 2 kPa leaves only about ${pct(2, "adult")} %.</li>
<li><strong>Affinity</strong> is a haemoglobin's tendency to bind oxygen. A curve to the <strong>left</strong> means higher affinity (loads at lower pO₂, unloads less readily); a curve to the <strong>right</strong> means lower affinity (unloads more readily). The pO₂ at which saturation is 50 % (P50) is a convenient measure.</li></ul>
<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s3">4. The effect of carbon dioxide: the Bohr effect <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Respiring tissues produce carbon dioxide. A higher <strong>pCO₂</strong> lowers the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen, shifting the dissociation curve to the <strong>right</strong>: at any given pO₂ haemoglobin holds less oxygen, so more is unloaded. This is the <strong>Bohr effect</strong>. The mechanism (<span class="badge">supporting</span>): CO₂ dissolves and reacts with water to form carbonic acid, which releases hydrogen ions; the H⁺ ions bind to haemoglobin and change its shape, reducing its affinity for oxygen. The result is beautifully matched to need: the most active tissues produce the most CO₂ and receive the most oxygen. In the lungs CO₂ is lost, pCO₂ is low, the curve shifts back to the left and loading is favoured.</p>
<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s4 ${R}.s5 ${R}.s6">5. Different haemoglobins for different ways of life <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Because the globin chains differ between species, so does the affinity of the haemoglobin, and natural selection has matched each to the organism's environment and activity. The guiding question is always: <em>where does this animal load oxygen, and how fast must it unload?</em></p>
<table><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Curve relative to adult human</th><th>Why it is an advantage</th></tr>
<tr><th>Mammalian fetus</th><td>Left (higher affinity)</td><td>Fetal blood meets maternal blood in the placenta, where pO₂ is already lowered; a higher affinity lets fetal haemoglobin take oxygen <em>from</em> the mother's haemoglobin at that pO₂. After birth it is replaced by adult haemoglobin, which unloads more readily.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Llama (high altitude)</th><td>Left</td><td>Atmospheric pO₂ is low at altitude, so a higher affinity is needed to become saturated in the lungs; unloading is still adequate because tissue pO₂ is lower too.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Lugworm (burrow in mud; low pO₂)</th><td>Far left</td><td>Loads at the very low pO₂ of water in its burrow; low activity means slow unloading is acceptable; the haemoglobin also acts as an oxygen store between tides.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Mouse (small, very active, high metabolic rate)</th><td>Right (lower affinity)</td><td>Small mammals lose heat fast and respire quickly; a right-shifted curve unloads oxygen rapidly to the tissues. Plenty of oxygen is available in the lungs, so loading is still high (about ${pct(13, "mouse")} % in the model, against ${pct(13, "adult")} % for human haemoglobin).</td></tr>
<tr><th>Dove or other active bird (<span class="badge">supporting</span>)</th><td>Right</td><td>High metabolic rate of flight muscles needs fast unloading.</td></tr></table>
<p>The pattern: <strong>low-oxygen environments select for high affinity</strong> (curve left, loading favoured); <strong>high metabolic rates select for lower affinity</strong> (curve right, unloading favoured). Each curve is a compromise between loading and unloading, and different species strike it differently; the curves differ in shape as well as position, so describe them as "shifted to the left/right at the pO₂ in question", not as identical copies. <span class="badge amber">extension</span> Myoglobin, the oxygen store in muscle, has one haem, no cooperative binding and a hyperbolic curve far to the left: it takes oxygen from haemoglobin and releases it only when muscle pO₂ is very low.</p>
<div class="notice amber"><strong>Common errors.</strong> Saying haemoglobin "carries carbon dioxide instead" in the Bohr effect (CO₂ lowers its affinity for O₂; most CO₂ is carried as hydrogencarbonate in plasma). Describing a left shift as "better": it is better for loading in low pO₂, worse for unloading. Confusing pO₂ (x-axis) with saturation (y-axis), or saying the fetus has a "higher pO₂" (it has a higher affinity). Naming vessels that are not required (renal, mesenteric) while forgetting the hepatic portal vein.</div>`,
    diagrams: [
      { id: "d-3.1.5.3-1", title: "General pattern of mammalian circulation", status: "reviewed", caption: "Double circulation with the heart, lungs, body, liver, gut and kidneys. The vessels whose names the specification requires are the vena cava, pulmonary artery and vein, aorta, coronary arteries, hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein and hepatic vein; the artery to the gut and the renal artery and vein are shown only to complete the circuit. Red = oxygenated, blue = deoxygenated. Schematic.", alt: "Block diagram of the circulation: the pulmonary artery leaves the right ventricle for the lungs and the pulmonary vein returns to the left atrium; the aorta leaves the left ventricle for the body, with branches to the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the liver (hepatic artery), the gut and the kidneys (renal artery); the gut drains to the liver through the hepatic portal vein; the hepatic vein, renal vein and veins from the body join the vena cava, which enters the right atrium.", svg: A.circulation() },
      { id: "d-3.1.5.3-2", title: "Red blood cell", status: "reviewed", caption: "Biconcave disc, about 7–8 µm across, no nucleus. Standard dimensions from histology texts.", alt: "A red blood cell face on and in section showing the biconcave shape.", svg: A.rbc() },
      { id: "d-3.1.5.3-3", title: "Haemoglobin: four globin chains, four haem groups", status: "reviewed", caption: "Each polypeptide globin chain is associated with an iron-containing haem group whose Fe²⁺ binds one O₂. Adult human haemoglobin has two α and two β chains. Shapes schematic.", alt: "Four lobes representing globin chains, each with a square haem group containing an iron ion; oxygen molecules nearby.", svg: A.hbMolecule() },
      { id: "d-3.1.5.3-4", title: "Oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve (adult human)", status: "reviewed", caption: "Percentage saturation against pO₂ in kPa from the documented Hill model (P50 ≈ 3.6 kPa, n ≈ 2.7). Loading in the lungs at about 13 kPa; unloading in respiring tissue at about 5 kPa. Model curve based on literature values, not a measured dataset.", alt: "S-shaped curve rising steeply between 2 and 8 kPa and levelling near 100 percent, with the lung and tissue points marked.", svg: A.odc({ variants: ["adult"] }) },
      { id: "d-3.1.5.3-5", title: "The Bohr effect", status: "reviewed", caption: "At a higher pCO₂ the curve lies to the right: at the same pO₂ haemoglobin is less saturated, so more oxygen is unloaded to the tissue. Model curves (Bohr coefficient −0.48 per pH unit).", alt: "Two S-shaped curves, the dashed one shifted to the right for higher carbon dioxide.", svg: A.odc({ variants: ["adult"], marks: false, extraCurves: [{ pts: M.range(0, 14, 70).map((x) => ({ x, y: 100 * M.sat(x, "adult", 9) })), color: "#b4504f", dash: "6 4", label: "pCO₂ 9 kPa", labelPos: { x: 8, y: 100 * M.sat(8, "adult", 9) - 8, a: "start" } }] }) },
      { id: "d-3.1.5.3-6", title: "Haemoglobins of different organisms", status: "reviewed", caption: "Fetal, llama and lugworm haemoglobins lie to the left of adult human (higher affinity); mouse lies to the right (lower affinity). Each curve has its own P50 and shape, from approximate published values (for example Severinghaus 1979 for adult human haemoglobin); they are illustrative, not clinical data.", alt: "Several S-shaped curves at different positions, labelled by organism.", svg: A.odc({ variants: ["adult", "fetal", "llama", "lugworm", "mouse"], marks: false, width: 620 }) },
    ],
    interactives: [
      { kind: "odcExplorer", title: "Oxygen Dissociation Curve Explorer", note: "Read saturation at any pO₂, change the pCO₂ to see the Bohr shift, and overlay the haemoglobins of other organisms (each with its own documented P50 and Hill coefficient)." },
      { kind: "hbChallenge", title: "Haemoglobin Adaptation Challenge", note: "Approximate affinity data for organisms in different environments: identify the advantage of each haemoglobin and the trade-off it accepts.",
        cards: [
          { title: "Human fetus v adult (literature values, approximate)", html: "P50 of fetal haemoglobin ≈ 2.6 kPa; adult ≈ 3.6 kPa. Placental blood pO₂ ≈ 4–5 kPa. At 4 kPa the model gives fetal Hb " + pct(4, "fetal") + " % saturated and adult Hb " + pct(4, "adult") + " %." },
          { title: "Llama (Andes, 4000 m) v human at sea level (approximate)", html: "P50 llama ≈ 2.9 kPa; human ≈ 3.6 kPa. Alveolar pO₂ at 4000 m ≈ 6–7 kPa (sea level ≈ 13 kPa). At 6 kPa: llama Hb " + pct(6, "llama") + " % saturated; human " + pct(6, "adult") + " %." },
          { title: "Lugworm, Arenicola (burrow in tidal mud; literature value at 10 °C)", html: "P50 ≈ 0.25 kPa. Water in the burrow between tides may fall below 1 kPa pO₂. At 1 kPa: lugworm Hb " + pct(1, "lugworm") + " % saturated; human Hb " + pct(1, "adult") + " %. The worm is sluggish and spends hours without fresh water." },
          { title: "Mouse v human (approximate)", html: "P50 mouse ≈ 5.3 kPa; human ≈ 3.6 kPa. Metabolic rate per gram of a mouse is about 7 times a human's. At tissue pO₂ 5 kPa: mouse Hb " + pct(5, "mouse") + " % saturated; human " + pct(5, "adult") + " %." },
        ],
        questions: [
          { id: "q-3.1.5.3-hc1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s5", "3.1.5.3.s6"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Fetal haemoglobin. Use the card to explain why a higher affinity is essential for the fetus and why fetal haemoglobin is replaced after birth. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "In the placenta pO₂ is low (≈ 4–5 kPa) because maternal blood has already partly unloaded; fetal Hb must take oxygen from maternal Hb", marks: 1 }, { point: "At that pO₂ fetal Hb is more saturated than adult Hb (quote figures), so oxygen moves from mother to fetus", marks: 1 }, { point: "After birth the lungs provide a high pO₂ for loading, and a lower-affinity haemoglobin unloads oxygen to the tissues more readily", marks: 1 }], model: "The fetus obtains all its oxygen from the mother's blood in the placenta, where the pO₂ is only about 4–5 kPa because the maternal haemoglobin has already partly unloaded. Fetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity, so at 4 kPa it is about " + pct(4, "fetal") + " % saturated while adult haemoglobin is about " + pct(4, "adult") + " %: oxygen therefore passes from maternal to fetal haemoglobin. After birth the baby's lungs give a high pO₂ for loading, and a haemoglobin with a lower affinity is better because it unloads oxygen to the respiring tissues more readily.", commonErrors: "Saying the fetus has a higher pO₂." },
          { id: "q-3.1.5.3-hc2", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s6", "3.1.5.3.s5"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Llama. Explain the advantage of the llama's haemoglobin at altitude and the trade-off it involves. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Atmospheric / alveolar pO₂ is low at altitude, so adult human Hb would not become fully saturated in the lungs", marks: 1 }, { point: "The llama's higher-affinity (left-shifted) Hb reaches a higher saturation at 6 kPa (quote figures: " + pct(6, "llama") + " % v " + pct(6, "adult") + " %), so more oxygen is loaded", marks: 1 }, { point: "Trade-off: it holds oxygen more tightly, so unloading at tissue pO₂ is less complete; tissue pO₂ must fall lower to release the same amount", marks: 1 }], model: "At 4000 m the alveolar pO₂ is only about 6–7 kPa, on the steep part of the curve. At 6 kPa human haemoglobin is about " + pct(6, "adult") + " % saturated, while the llama's haemoglobin, which has a higher affinity and a curve to the left, is about " + pct(6, "llama") + " % saturated, so it loads more oxygen from thin air. The cost is that it also unloads less readily: at a given tissue pO₂ it retains more oxygen, so the tissues must run at a lower pO₂ to obtain the same supply.", commonErrors: "Describing the llama's curve as 'better' without the trade-off." },
          { id: "q-3.1.5.3-hc3", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s6"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Lugworm. Explain how the lugworm's haemoglobin suits its environment and way of life. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Burrow water has a very low pO₂, especially between tides", marks: 1 }, { point: "Very high affinity (very low P50, curve far left) lets the haemoglobin load even at pO₂ below 1 kPa (quote figures)", marks: 1 }, { point: "The worm has a low metabolic rate / is sluggish, so slow unloading at even lower pO₂ is sufficient; the haemoglobin also stores oxygen between tides", marks: 1 }], model: "A lugworm lives in a burrow in tidal mud where the water, especially between tides, has a very low pO₂. Its haemoglobin has an extremely high affinity for oxygen, with a P50 of about 0.25 kPa, so even at 1 kPa it is almost fully saturated while human haemoglobin would be only " + pct(1, "adult") + " % saturated. Because the worm is sluggish with a low metabolic rate, oxygen need only be released slowly at the still lower pO₂ in its tissues, and the loaded haemoglobin acts as a store during the hours when no fresh water reaches the burrow.", commonErrors: "Not linking affinity to the low pO₂ of the habitat." },
          { id: "q-3.1.5.3-hc4", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s6"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Mouse. Explain why a right-shifted curve suits a mouse, and why it is not a disadvantage in the lungs. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "High metabolic rate per gram (heat loss from a large SA : V) means tissues need oxygen delivered rapidly", marks: 1 }, { point: "Lower affinity means more oxygen is unloaded at tissue pO₂ (quote figures: " + pct(5, "mouse") + " % v " + pct(5, "adult") + " % at 5 kPa)", marks: 1 }, { point: "At the high pO₂ of the lungs even a right-shifted curve is still highly saturated (about " + pct(13, "mouse") + " % v " + pct(13, "adult") + " %), so loading is only slightly reduced", marks: 1 }], model: "A mouse has a very large surface area to volume ratio, loses heat quickly and has a metabolic rate per gram about seven times a human's, so its tissues need oxygen delivered fast. Its haemoglobin has a lower affinity (curve to the right), so at a tissue pO₂ of 5 kPa it is only about " + pct(5, "mouse") + " % saturated compared with " + pct(5, "adult") + " % for human haemoglobin: it unloads a much larger share of its oxygen. In the lungs the pO₂ is high enough that even this haemoglobin is still about " + pct(13, "mouse") + " % saturated, against " + pct(13, "adult") + " % for human haemoglobin, so the shift costs little in loading.", commonErrors: "Saying the mouse's haemoglobin is 'worse'." },
          { id: "q-3.1.5.3-hc5", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s6"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "A newly studied fish lives in fast, cold, well-oxygenated streams and is highly active. Which haemoglobin property would you predict?", options: ["Very high affinity, curve far to the left", "Relatively low affinity, curve to the right, favouring rapid unloading", "A hyperbolic curve", "No haemoglobin"], answer: 1, explanation: "Plenty of oxygen to load; activity demands fast unloading." },
        ] },
      { kind: "circulationExplorer", title: "Mammalian Circulation Explorer", note: "Step through one complete double circuit, vessel by vessel, and see where the blood becomes oxygenated and deoxygenated; click any vessel or organ to identify it." },
    ],
    focus: [
      { slot: "haemoglobin-structure", title: "Haemoglobin, the molecule", ref: R, explanation: "<p>This rendering of human haemoglobin, from X-ray crystallography data, shows the four globin chains (two α in one colour, two β in another) each cradling a haem group. The haem's iron ion is where oxygen binds. The chains are held together only by weak interactions, which is exactly what allows cooperative binding: when one haem takes oxygen the chains shift relative to one another, opening the other haems. Haemoglobins across the animal kingdom share this plan, globin chains with haem, but differ in the amino-acid sequence of the chains, and those differences set the affinity.</p>", question: { id: "q-3.1.5.3-fx1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s4", "3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Haemoglobin has a quaternary structure. Explain what this means and why it is important for the way haemoglobin transports oxygen. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Quaternary structure: more than one polypeptide chain (four globin chains) held together", marks: 1 }, { point: "Each chain has a haem group with Fe²⁺ that binds one O₂, so one molecule carries four", marks: 1 }, { point: "Binding of one O₂ changes the shape of the molecule, making further binding easier (cooperative binding): gives the S-shaped curve, efficient loading in the lungs and unloading in tissues", marks: 1 }], model: "Quaternary structure means the protein is made of more than one polypeptide chain: haemoglobin has four globin chains, each with an iron-containing haem group that binds one oxygen molecule, so each molecule carries up to four. When the first oxygen binds, the chains shift relative to one another and the remaining haems become easier to reach, so binding is cooperative. This gives the S-shaped dissociation curve that lets haemoglobin load almost fully in the lungs yet release a large fraction of its oxygen over the small drop in pO₂ found in respiring tissues.", commonErrors: "Confusing quaternary with tertiary structure." } },
    ],
    examFocus: `<ul><li>Vessels: learn the eight names in the table and nothing more; the examiners' phrase is "entering and leaving the heart and liver".</li><li>Describe the curve in words: S-shaped; steep between about 3 and 8 kPa; plateau above 10 kPa; explain the shape by cooperative binding; relate the lungs to the top and tissues to the steep part.</li><li>Always read and quote values from a given curve ("at 4 kPa, raising pCO₂ from 5.3 to 9 kPa lowers saturation from ${pct(4, "adult")} % to ${pct(4, "adult", 9)} %"), then interpret.</li><li>Bohr effect: higher pCO₂ → lower affinity → curve to the right → more unloading where respiration is fastest.</li><li>Organism questions: name the environment or activity, say whether loading or unloading matters more, then state the direction of the shift and its trade-off.</li></ul>`,
    synoptic: [
      { ref: "3.1.1.4", text: "Haemoglobin is a globular protein with quaternary structure; cooperative binding is a change in tertiary and quaternary structure." },
      { ref: "3.1.5.2", text: "Loading happens at the alveoli, where ventilation keeps pO₂ high and pCO₂ low." },
      { ref: "3.1.5.1", text: "Blood is the mass transport system that carries oxygen to within a short diffusion distance of every cell." },
      { ref: "3.2.6.2", text: "Heart structure and the cardiac cycle pump the blood around this circuit." },
    ],
    quickCheck: [
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-qc1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which vessel carries oxygenated blood?", options: ["Pulmonary artery", "Vena cava", "Pulmonary vein", "Hepatic vein"], answer: 2, explanation: "Lungs → left atrium." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-qc2", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s4"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Each haem group of haemoglobin contains:", options: ["a copper ion", "an iron ion that binds one O₂", "a magnesium ion", "a polypeptide"], answer: 1, explanation: "Fe²⁺ in haem." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-qc3", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: "Complete the passage.", text: "In the lungs, where the partial pressure of oxygen is {{1}}, haemoglobin {{2}} oxygen. In respiring tissues, where it is low, haemoglobin {{3}} oxygen.", answers: { 1: ["high"], 2: ["loads", "associates with", "binds"], 3: ["unloads", "releases", "dissociates from"] }, explanation: "High; loads; unloads." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-qc4", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s3"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "A rise in carbon dioxide concentration:", options: ["increases the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen", "shifts the dissociation curve to the right, so more oxygen is unloaded", "shifts the curve to the left", "has no effect on haemoglobin"], answer: 1, explanation: "Bohr effect." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-qc5", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s5", "3.1.5.3.s6"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "An organism living where oxygen is scarce would be expected to have haemoglobin with:", options: ["a lower affinity than human haemoglobin", "a higher affinity, curve to the left", "no haem groups", "a curve to the right"], answer: 1, explanation: "Loading at low pO₂ needs high affinity." },
    ],
    flashcards: [
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-1", front: "What is a double circulation?", back: "Blood passes through the heart twice per circuit: pulmonary circuit (right side → lungs → left side) and systemic circuit (left side → body → right side)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-2", front: "Arteries, capillaries, veins: direction and role.", back: "Arteries carry blood away from the heart (high pressure); capillaries exchange with tissues (walls one cell thick); veins return blood to the heart (low pressure)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-3", front: "Name the vessels entering and leaving the heart.", back: "Entering: vena cava (right atrium), pulmonary vein (left atrium). Leaving: pulmonary artery (right ventricle), aorta (left ventricle)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-4", front: "Name the vessels entering and leaving the liver.", back: "Entering: hepatic artery (from aorta, oxygenated) and hepatic portal vein (from gut, nutrient-rich). Leaving: hepatic vein (to vena cava)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-5", front: "What do the coronary arteries do?", back: "Branch from the aorta just above the heart and supply the heart muscle with oxygenated blood." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-6", front: "Which artery carries deoxygenated blood and which vein carries oxygenated blood?", back: "Pulmonary artery (to lungs) is deoxygenated; pulmonary vein (from lungs) is oxygenated." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-7", front: "Why is the hepatic portal vein unusual?", back: "It carries blood from one capillary bed (gut) to another (liver), so absorbed nutrients pass through the liver first." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-8", front: "Three adaptations of red blood cells for oxygen transport.", back: "Biconcave shape (large SA : V, short diffusion distance); no nucleus (more haemoglobin); small and flexible (pass capillaries in single file close to the wall)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-9", front: "Describe the structure of haemoglobin (spec statement 4).", back: "A protein of polypeptide globin chains (four in adult human: 2α, 2β), each associated with an iron-containing haem group; each Fe²⁺ binds one O₂." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-10", front: "What are loading and unloading?", back: "Loading (association): haemoglobin binds O₂ at high pO₂ (lungs). Unloading (dissociation): releases O₂ at low pO₂ (respiring tissues)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-11", front: "What does the oxygen dissociation curve plot?", back: "Percentage saturation of haemoglobin with oxygen (y) against partial pressure of oxygen, pO₂ in kPa (x)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-12", front: "Why is the dissociation curve S-shaped?", back: "Cooperative binding: the first O₂ binds with difficulty, changes the shape of haemoglobin and makes the next haems easier to load (steep part); the last is harder (plateau)." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-13", front: "Why is the steep part of the curve useful?", back: "It covers tissue pO₂ (3–8 kPa): a small fall in pO₂ unloads a large amount of oxygen where it is needed." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-14", front: "What does a curve to the left mean? To the right?", back: "Left: higher affinity, loads at lower pO₂, unloads less readily. Right: lower affinity, unloads more readily." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-15", front: "State the Bohr effect.", back: "A higher pCO₂ lowers the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen, shifting the curve to the right so more oxygen is unloaded in actively respiring tissue." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-16", front: "Mechanism of the Bohr effect (supporting).", back: "CO₂ forms carbonic acid, releasing H⁺; H⁺ binds haemoglobin and alters its shape, reducing affinity for O₂." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-17", front: "Why does fetal haemoglobin have a higher affinity?", back: "It must take oxygen from maternal haemoglobin in the placenta, where pO₂ is already low; a left-shifted curve is more saturated at that pO₂." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-18", front: "Llama haemoglobin: direction and reason.", back: "Left-shifted (higher affinity): loads well at the low atmospheric pO₂ of high altitude." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-19", front: "Lugworm haemoglobin: direction and reason.", back: "Far left (very high affinity): loads at the very low pO₂ of burrow water; low activity so slow unloading suffices; stores oxygen between tides." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-20", front: "Small active mammal (mouse): direction and reason.", back: "Right-shifted (lower affinity): high metabolic rate needs rapid unloading; loading is still high (about 90 %) at lung pO₂." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-21", front: "General rule linking environment to haemoglobin.", back: "Low external pO₂ → higher affinity (left). High metabolic rate → lower affinity (right). Each is a compromise between loading and unloading." },
      { id: "f-3.1.5.3-22", front: "What is P50?", back: "The pO₂ at which haemoglobin is 50 % saturated: a measure of affinity (lower P50 = higher affinity)." },
    ],
    questions: [
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Blood leaving the right ventricle enters the:", options: ["aorta", "pulmonary artery", "pulmonary vein", "vena cava"], answer: 1, explanation: "To the lungs." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m2", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which vessel carries blood from the gut to the liver?", options: ["Hepatic artery", "Hepatic vein", "Hepatic portal vein", "Vena cava"], answer: 2, explanation: "Portal: between two capillary beds." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m3", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "The heart muscle receives its oxygen from:", options: ["blood in the ventricles", "the coronary arteries", "the pulmonary veins", "the vena cava"], answer: 1, explanation: "Branches of the aorta." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m4", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s4"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Haemoglobin is described as having quaternary structure because:", options: ["it contains iron", "it is made of four polypeptide chains", "it binds four oxygen molecules", "it is found in red blood cells"], answer: 1, explanation: "Quaternary = more than one chain." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m5", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s4", "3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "One molecule of adult human haemoglobin can carry a maximum of:", options: ["one O₂", "two O₂", "four O₂", "eight O₂"], answer: 2, explanation: "One per haem, four haems." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m6", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "The partial pressure of oxygen is:", options: ["the pressure due to oxygen alone within a mixture of gases; a measure of oxygen concentration", "the total air pressure", "the percentage saturation of haemoglobin", "the volume of oxygen in the blood"], answer: 0, explanation: "pO₂, in kPa." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m7", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "On the dissociation curve, the region where a small fall in pO₂ causes a large fall in saturation is:", options: ["the plateau at high pO₂", "the steep part at intermediate pO₂", "the origin", "above 100 %"], answer: 1, explanation: "Where tissues operate." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m8", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Cooperative binding means that:", options: ["all four O₂ bind at once", "binding one O₂ changes the shape of haemoglobin so the next binds more easily", "haemoglobin binds CO₂ and O₂ together", "two haemoglobin molecules bind each other"], answer: 1, explanation: "Explains the S shape." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m9", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s3"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "In actively respiring muscle, pCO₂ is high. The effect on haemoglobin is:", options: ["increased affinity and more loading", "decreased affinity and more unloading", "no change", "haemoglobin denatures"], answer: 1, explanation: "Bohr effect delivers oxygen where needed." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m10", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s5"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "The dissociation curve of fetal haemoglobin lies to the left of adult haemoglobin. This means fetal haemoglobin:", options: ["has a lower affinity for oxygen", "is more saturated at any given pO₂", "unloads oxygen more easily", "cannot bind oxygen"], answer: 1, explanation: "Higher affinity." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m11", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s6", "3.1.5.3.s5"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which organism would you expect to have haemoglobin with the lowest affinity for oxygen?", options: ["A lugworm in a burrow", "A llama at 4000 m", "A shrew (tiny, very active mammal)", "A human fetus"], answer: 2, explanation: "High metabolic rate favours unloading." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m12", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Red blood cells have no nucleus. The main advantage is:", options: ["they can divide faster", "more space for haemoglobin", "they can respire faster", "they are larger"], answer: 1, explanation: "Maximises oxygen carriage." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m13", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which statement about the pulmonary vein is correct?", options: ["It carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs", "It carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium", "It leaves the right ventricle", "It joins the vena cava"], answer: 1, explanation: "The exception among veins." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-m14", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Using the model curve, adult haemoglobin at 13 kPa is about " + pct(13, "adult") + " % saturated and at 5 kPa about " + pct(5, "adult") + " %. Between the lungs and tissue at 5 kPa, the percentage saturation therefore falls by about:", options: [pct(5, "adult") + " percentage points", (pct(13, "adult") - pct(5, "adult")) + " percentage points", pct(13, "adult") + " percentage points", "5 percentage points"], answer: 1, explanation: pct(13, "adult") + " − " + pct(5, "adult") + " = " + (pct(13, "adult") - pct(5, "adult")) + " percentage points. As a share of the oxygen loaded in the lungs this is " + (pct(13, "adult") - pct(5, "adult")) + " ÷ " + pct(13, "adult") + " × 100 ≈ " + Math.round(100 * (pct(13, "adult") - pct(5, "adult")) / pct(13, "adult")) + " %." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-g1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s4", "3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "gap", marks: 4, stem: "Complete the passage.", text: "Haemoglobin consists of four {{1}} chains, each with an iron-containing {{2}} group. Oxygen binds to the {{3}} ion in each group. The binding of the first oxygen makes it {{4}} for the next to bind.", answers: { 1: ["globin", "polypeptide"], 2: ["haem"], 3: ["iron", "Fe2+", "Fe²⁺"], 4: ["easier"] }, explanation: "Globin; haem; iron; easier (cooperative binding)." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-g2", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: "Complete the passage on circulation.", text: "Blood returns from the body to the right atrium in the {{1}}, is pumped to the lungs through the {{2}}, and returns to the left atrium through the {{3}}.", answers: { 1: ["vena cava"], 2: ["pulmonary artery"], 3: ["pulmonary vein"] }, explanation: "Vena cava; pulmonary artery; pulmonary vein." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-lab1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "label", drag: true, marks: 6, stem: "Label the six numbered vessels on the circulation diagram.", svg: (() => { const base = A.circulation(); const num = (x, y, n) => `<circle cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="12" fill="#1e3a5f"/><text x="${x}" y="${y + 4}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#fff" font-weight="700">${n}</text>`; return base.replace(/<text[^>]*font-size="10.5"[^>]*fill="(#b4504f|#1e3a5f)"[^>]*>[^<]*<\/text>/g, "").replace("</svg>", num(236, 160, 1) + num(430, 150, 2) + num(430, 345, 3) + num(250, 300, 4) + num(135, 396, 5) + num(231, 338, 6) + "</svg>"); })(), labels: [{ n: 1, answer: "Pulmonary artery" }, { n: 2, answer: "Pulmonary vein" }, { n: 3, answer: "Aorta" }, { n: 4, answer: "Vena cava" }, { n: 5, answer: "Hepatic portal vein" }, { n: 6, answer: "Hepatic vein" }], bank: ["Pulmonary artery", "Pulmonary vein", "Aorta", "Vena cava", "Hepatic portal vein", "Hepatic vein", "Hepatic artery", "Renal artery"] },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-match1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s5", "3.1.5.3.s6"], type: "match", marks: 4, stem: "Match each organism to the position of its dissociation curve relative to adult human haemoglobin and the reason.", pairs: [{ left: "Human fetus", right: "Left: must take oxygen from maternal blood at low placental pO₂" }, { left: "Llama", right: "Left: low atmospheric pO₂ at high altitude" }, { left: "Lugworm", right: "Far left: very low pO₂ in burrow water; low activity" }, { left: "Mouse", right: "Right: high metabolic rate needs rapid unloading" }] },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-d1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "short", marks: 4, stem: "Data. From a dissociation curve, a student read these saturations for adult haemoglobin: 2 kPa 17 %; 4 kPa 58 %; 6 kPa 80 %; 8 kPa 90 %; 13 kPa 97 %. (a) Describe the shape of the curve, quoting data. (2 marks) (b) Resting tissue has a pO₂ of about 6 kPa and exercising muscle about 2 kPa. Use the data to explain how the curve ensures exercising muscle receives more oxygen. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "S-shaped / sigmoidal: steep between about 2 and 6 kPa (17 → 80 %)", marks: 1 }, { point: "Flattens at high pO₂ (90 → 97 % between 8 and 13 kPa)", marks: 1 }, { point: "At 6 kPa haemoglobin still holds 80 %; at 2 kPa only 17 %", marks: 1 }, { point: "So exercising muscle receives an extra 63 percentage points of the oxygen carried: the steep part means a modest fall in pO₂ unloads a great deal", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) The curve is sigmoidal: it rises steeply in the middle, from 17 % at 2 kPa to 80 % at 6 kPa, then flattens, rising only from 90 % to 97 % between 8 and 13 kPa. (b) In resting tissue at 6 kPa haemoglobin remains 80 % saturated, so it has released only about 17 % of its load, but in exercising muscle at 2 kPa it is 17 % saturated and has released about 80 %. Because exercising muscle lies on the steep part of the curve, its lower pO₂ unloads a great deal more oxygen, matching supply to demand.", commonErrors: "Describing the shape without data; confusing saturation with amount unloaded." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-d2", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s3"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Data. At a pO₂ of 5 kPa, haemoglobin saturation was 71 % when pCO₂ was 5.3 kPa, and 56 % when pCO₂ was 9 kPa (model values). Name or describe the effect, state what the figures show, and explain its importance. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Identifies the effect of carbon dioxide on oxygen transport by haemoglobin (the name Bohr effect is accepted but not required)", marks: 1 }, { point: "At the same pO₂, higher pCO₂ lowers saturation by 15 percentage points: affinity reduced / curve shifted right", marks: 1 }, { point: "More oxygen is unloaded in tissues respiring fastest, where CO₂ production is greatest", marks: 1 }], model: "This is the Bohr effect. At the same pO₂ of 5 kPa, raising the pCO₂ from 5.3 to 9 kPa lowers the saturation from 71 % to 56 %: the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen has fallen and the curve has shifted to the right. Its importance is that the tissues producing the most carbon dioxide are the ones respiring fastest, and they therefore receive the most oxygen.", commonErrors: "Saying CO₂ 'pushes oxygen off'." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-s1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s1"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Describe the pathway taken by a red blood cell from the vena cava to the aorta, naming the vessels and chambers it passes through. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle", marks: 1 }, { point: "Pulmonary artery → lungs (capillaries, oxygenated) → pulmonary vein", marks: 1 }, { point: "Left atrium → left ventricle → aorta", marks: 1 }], model: "From the vena cava the cell enters the right atrium and passes into the right ventricle, which pumps it through the pulmonary artery to the lungs. In the lung capillaries its haemoglobin loads oxygen, and it returns in the pulmonary vein to the left atrium, then the left ventricle, which pumps it into the aorta.", commonErrors: "Sending blood from the right ventricle into the aorta." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-s2", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Explain how the structure of a red blood cell is related to its function. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Biconcave disc: large surface area to volume ratio for diffusion of oxygen in and out", marks: 1 }, { point: "No nucleus (or organelles): more space for haemoglobin / short diffusion distance to all haemoglobin", marks: 1 }, { point: "Small and flexible: passes through narrow capillaries in single file, close to the wall", marks: 1 }], model: "The biconcave disc shape gives a large surface area to volume ratio, so oxygen diffuses quickly in and out, and keeps every haemoglobin molecule close to the surface. Having no nucleus leaves more room for haemoglobin. The cell is small and flexible, so it can squeeze through capillaries in single file, pressed against the wall where the diffusion distance to the tissue or alveolus is shortest.", commonErrors: "Saying red cells have a large surface area without the ratio." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-s3", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2"], type: "short", marks: 4, stem: "Explain why the oxygen dissociation curve is S-shaped and how this shape makes haemoglobin an efficient oxygen carrier. (4 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "First O₂ binds with difficulty (haems hidden / low affinity at low pO₂)", marks: 1 }, { point: "Binding changes the tertiary/quaternary structure, making the other haems more accessible: cooperative binding, steep rise", marks: 1 }, { point: "Last O₂ harder to bind as sites fill: plateau", marks: 1 }, { point: "Efficient: near-full loading at lung pO₂ (plateau) yet large unloading over the small pO₂ range of tissues (steep part)", marks: 1 }], model: "At low pO₂ the first oxygen molecule binds with difficulty because the haem groups are hidden within the folded protein. Once it binds, the shape of the molecule changes and the remaining haems become more accessible, so the second and third molecules bind much more easily: this cooperative binding produces the steep middle of the curve. The fourth is harder to bind because only one site remains, so the curve flattens. The shape makes haemoglobin efficient: on the plateau it loads almost completely at the pO₂ of the lungs, yet on the steep part a small fall in pO₂ in respiring tissue releases a large fraction of that oxygen.", commonErrors: "Explaining the shape as 'because haemoglobin has four sites' without the shape change." },
      { id: "q-3.1.5.3-e1", ref: R, stmts: ["3.1.5.3.s2", "3.1.5.3.s3"], type: "extended", marks: 6, stem: "Extended response. Explain how the properties of haemoglobin and the effect of carbon dioxide ensure that oxygen is loaded in the lungs and delivered to the tissues in proportion to their needs. (6 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Lungs: high pO₂, haemoglobin has high affinity / loads to near saturation (plateau of the curve)", marks: 1 }, { point: "Low pCO₂ in lungs (CO₂ lost to air) keeps affinity high, favouring loading", marks: 1 }, { point: "Tissues: lower pO₂, so haemoglobin unloads oxygen; cooperative binding makes release easy once started", marks: 1 }, { point: "Tissue pO₂ lies on the steep part of the curve, so a small fall in pO₂ unloads a large amount of oxygen", marks: 1 }, { point: "Respiring tissue produces CO₂, which lowers the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen, so the curve shifts right (credit, not required: the carbonic acid / H⁺ mechanism and the name Bohr effect)", marks: 1 }, { point: "So at the same pO₂ more oxygen is unloaded; the more active the tissue, the higher its pCO₂ and the lower its pO₂, the more oxygen it receives", marks: 1 }], levels: "Level 3 (5–6): loading and unloading both explained with the curve, Bohr effect with mechanism and its matching to demand. Level 2 (3–4): both explained without full linkage. Level 1 (1–2): fragments.", model: "In the alveoli the partial pressure of oxygen is high, about 13 kPa, and haemoglobin has a high affinity for oxygen here, so it loads to almost complete saturation on the plateau of the dissociation curve. Carbon dioxide is lost to the alveolar air, so pCO₂ is low, which keeps the affinity high and favours loading. In respiring tissues the pO₂ is much lower, on the steep part of the curve, so haemoglobin unloads a large proportion of its oxygen; because binding is cooperative, once one molecule is released the rest leave more easily. The tissues also produce carbon dioxide, which forms carbonic acid and releases hydrogen ions; these bind to haemoglobin and reduce its affinity, shifting the curve to the right, the Bohr effect, so that at the same pO₂ still more oxygen is unloaded. The most active tissues have the lowest pO₂ and the highest pCO₂, so they receive the most oxygen: delivery is matched to need.", commonErrors: "Omitting the Bohr effect or giving it without linking to demand." },
    ],
  };
})();
